    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Quasiparticles known as Z4 parafermions are a strong qubit candidate for topological quantum computing, which would allow for greater robustness to error. Related Z2 parafermions (Majorana fermions) are known to emerge at the boundary of the Kitaev chain – a 1D charge-2e superconductor. Motivated by this, we suggest that a 1D charge-4e superconductor might be a natural system to look for Z4 parafermions. In this paper, we use a combination of numerical and analytical techniques to investigate the zero-temperature phase diagram of such a system. Specifically, our system is the 1D Fermi-Hubbard model with chemical potential and a charge-4e superconducting term involving neighboring sites. We first present some analytical results in the strongly coupled limits of our system, finding just trivial phases but some possibility of topological phases emerging. We then present a small region of the phase diagram in the nonperturbative regime mapped out using numerical DMRG (density matrix renormalization group) techniques, indicating a symmetry-breaking transition from Z4 to Z2, along with a potential topological phase transition. This study seeks to advance our understanding of where useful quasiparticles might emerge, and additionally furthers our understanding of exotic charge-4e superconductors – a generically hard problem to approach analytically.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Robertson, M. D. (2024). Mapping Out the Zero-Temperature Phase Diagram of a 1D Charge-4e Superconductor. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at https://purl.stanford.edu/wh755zt9322. https://doi.org/10.25740/wh755zt9322.",
            "type": "preferred citation",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        }
